issues.problematic_code_snippet:
if (!this._serverToken || this._isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)) {
    const tokenData = this._generateToken({name, expiresInMinutes});
    this._serverToken = tokenData;
    this._serverTokenExp = tokenData.exp;
}

_isJWTExpired(token, bufferSeconds = 300) {
    try {
        const decoded = jwt.decode(token);
        if (typeof decoded !== 'object' || !decoded.exp) {
            return true;
        }
        const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
        const timeRemaining = decoded.exp - now;
        return timeRemaining < bufferSeconds;

if (!this._serverToken || this._isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)) {
    const tokenData = this._generateToken({name, expiresInMinutes});
    this._serverToken = tokenData;
    this._serverTokenExp = tokenData.exp;
}

findings.body:
In `getToken()`, the cache now stores the whole return value of `_generateToken()` instead of its `token` field:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="94-104"}

When JWT auth is on, `getToken()` now returns `{token: {token: '<jwt>', exp}, exp}`. Downstream:

- `stats/utils/tinybird.js:33-34` reads `tokenData?.token` and puts it in the `Authorization` header, which becomes `Bearer [object Object]`. Every server-side Tinybird query fails auth.
- `api/endpoints/tinybird.js:17-22` returns `token: tokenData.token` to the admin client, so the stats app gets an object where it expects a JWT string.

Caching breaks too. On the next call, `_isJWTExpired(this._serverToken)` passes the object to `jwt.decode`, which returns `null`. Reading `null.exp` then throws inside the `try`, the `catch` returns `true`, and a new JWT is signed on every request.

::callout{type="warning"}
None of the current unit tests catch this. They only check `assert.ok(tokenResult.token)` and `notEqual` between results, and both pass with object references.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="98-98"}
                this._serverToken = tokenData.token;

The PR is described as a Tinybird filter cleanup, but it also changes how JWTs are signed and checked:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="147-148"}

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="162-174"}

- Dropping `{noTimestamp: true}` adds an `iat` claim to every token. That changes the payload Tinybird receives and was presumably excluded on purpose. Nothing in this PR explains why it now needs to be included.
- Switching `jwt.verify` to `jwt.decode` means `_isJWTExpired` no longer checks the signature. Today it only sees tokens Ghost signed itself, so the immediate risk is low. But the helper now accepts any well-formed token with a future `exp` as valid. For example, if the admin token rotates, a cached token signed with the old secret is kept instead of being regenerated.

Unless there's a reason that isn't documented here, revert both and keep this PR limited to the filter removal.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="147-147"}
        const token = jwt.sign(payload, this.tinybirdConfig.adminToken, {noTimestamp: true});

`ghost/core/core/server/data/tinybird/ARCHITECTURE.md` lines 253-256 still list `api_top_browsers`, `api_top_devices` and `api_top_os` as available endpoints. Those pipes no longer exist in `endpoints/`, and this PR removes them from the JWT scopes.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/tinybird/TinybirdService.js" lines="47-59"}

It's worth updating the doc in this PR so the cleanup is complete.
